<h2>Sensory Overload vs. Real Danger: The Medina Mindset</h2>
      <p>Before looking at statistics, it helps to understand why Western travelers frequently feel uneasy during their first twenty-four hours in Morocco. It is almost never because of actual peril—it is because of sensory shock.</p>
      <p>When you step into the ancient walled medina of Marrakech or Fes, the stimuli arrive completely unfiltered. A 50cc moped roars past your elbow through an alleyway barely six feet wide; three carpet vendors greet you in Spanish, French, and English; the scent of cedar shavings collides with roasting cumin and raw leather; the call to prayer reverberates simultaneously from five separate minarets. Your nervous system, conditioned by the predictable order of Western suburbs or commuter corridors, interprets this density as a threat. But notice what is actually happening around you: people are working, transporting fresh bread on wooden planks, greeting neighbors, haggling over olives, and living their daily lives. Density is not hostility.</p>

      <div class="roam-field-note">
        <span class="roam-field-note-kicker">Field Note // Derb Dabachi, Marrakech</span>
        <p class="roam-field-note-quote">&ldquo;If you get turned around in the narrow alleys near Ben Youssef and a teenager steps up offering to show you the way to Jemaa el-Fna, your instinct might be panic. But he isn&rsquo;t planning to harm you—he is an opportunist looking for a 20-dirham tip. Place your right hand gently over your heart, make calm eye contact, and say &lsquo;La, Shukran&rsquo; (&lsquo;No, thank you&rsquo;). Keep your shoulders relaxed and keep walking toward daylight. Morocco operates on mutual dignity and respect; panic attracts persistence, while calm, courteous resolve dissolves it instantly.&rdquo;</p>
        <p class="roam-field-note-author">&mdash; Hamza &amp; Abdelhakim, Co-Founders of Roam Morocco</p>
      </div>

      
